<section class="shell page-intro legal"><p class="eyebrow">UNDERSTANDING BEFORE ASSUMPTION</p><h1>About AC to DC Converter</h1><div class="prose">
 <h2>Who we are</h2><p>AC to DC Converter is the publishing identity for this calculator website and its educational guides. The content uses the organizational byline “AC to DC Converter editorial”. The site owner maintains its content and software through a private dashboard and the public source repository. Our <a href="/authors/">authors and editorial team page</a> explains these responsibilities without implying an unnamed staff of engineers.</p>
 <h2>Publishing identity and affiliations</h2><p>The website is published under the AC to DC Converter name, with source maintained in the <a href="https://github.com/actodcconverter1-ai/actodcconverter">actodcconverter GitHub repository</a>. No parent-company relationship, university affiliation, manufacturer endorsement or professional accreditation is represented. Linked manufacturers are technical sources, not partners or sponsors. The brand name and organizational byline do not establish a registered company or a licensed engineering service.</p>
 <h2>Our story</h2><p>This project developed around a common calculation problem: “AC to DC” can mean an unfiltered rectifier average, a capacitor-filtered voltage estimate or a regulated output. Those are different quantities and require different assumptions. The English guide collection began in November 2026. Development then added dedicated half-wave and full-wave reference tools, worked examples, contextual guides and translated pages. The <a href="https://github.com/actodcconverter1-ai/actodcconverter/commits/main/">public change history</a> records the project's software and content revisions. This is a young educational resource; it does not claim decades of operating history or laboratory experience.</p>
 <h2>What we do</h2><p>We provide browser-based calculators and explanations for rectification, filtering, regulation, voltage measurement and component assumptions. The main workbench separates fixed-diode-drop rectification, capacitor filtering with current or resistance loads, and conditional downstream regulator analysis. Dedicated ideal tools provide zero-drop analytical reference cases. Calculations run on your device. We do not design a complete power supply, certify equipment, sell components or determine the available current from an AC voltage label.</p>
 <h2>Evidence you can check</h2><p>Trust should come from reproducible information. The <a href="/ac-to-dc-calculator/#methodology">calculator methodology</a> states the equations and excluded behavior. The <a href="/blog/ac-to-dc-calculator-worked-examples/">worked examples</a> provide input values and results you can reproduce. The <a href="https://github.com/actodcconverter1-ai/actodcconverter/tree/main/tests">public software tests</a> compare analytical results with independent numerical integration, boundary cases and invalid inputs. These are software checks, not physical circuit measurements or an independent professional review.</p>
 <h2>Technical sources and attribution</h2><p>Guides link relevant manufacturer documents beside the explanation they support. Examples include <a href="https://www.ti.com/lit/ug/tidub95/tidub95.pdf">Texas Instruments' rectification and filtering reference</a> and <a href="https://www.keysight.com/us/en/assets/7018-01113/application-notes/5988-6916.pdf">Keysight's AC RMS measurement guidance</a>. Citing a document does not imply that its publisher reviewed or endorsed this website. Read the <a href="/editorial/">editorial standards</a> for source selection, automated assistance, translation and correction practices.</p>
 <h2>Contact and corrections</h2><p>The <a href="/contact/">private contact form</a> is the public route for questions, content corrections and rights-related concerns. A technical report should include the page URL, model, inputs with units, observed result, expected result and supporting source. We do not publish private login contact details. No office address, phone support service, team photographs, customer testimonials or awards are presented without verifiable information.</p>
